1
00:00:02,214 --> 00:00:04,877
So let's now dig a bit deeper and let's

2
00:00:04,958 --> 00:00:07,477
start with variables, constants, and

3
00:00:07,557 --> 00:00:09,851
properties. How should these be named?

4
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:13,718
We can differentiate between 3 main kinds of

5
00:00:13,911 --> 00:00:15,900
values which are being stored in them.

6
00:00:16,863 --> 00:00:19,446
Our values could be objects and this

7
00:00:19,446 --> 00:00:22,527
includes lists or maps or any other more

8
00:00:22,656 --> 00:00:24,100
complex data structure.

9
00:00:24,886 --> 00:00:27,485
Our value could be a simple number or

10
00:00:27,485 --> 00:00:27,918
string.

11
00:00:28,577 --> 00:00:30,328
Or our value could be a Boolean.

12
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:32,978
And you can already tell by the color coding

13
00:00:33,010 --> 00:00:34,938
here that there seems to be a difference

14
00:00:34,938 --> 00:00:35,324
between

15
00:00:35,902 --> 00:00:38,119
Booleans and the other kinds of values.

16
00:00:38,778 --> 00:00:41,107
Now, if your value which you store in a

17
00:00:41,107 --> 00:00:44,063
variable, constant, or property is an object

18
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,392
or an object-like thing,

19
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:49,252
then the name which you assign to your

20
00:00:49,396 --> 00:00:52,288
variable or property should simply describe

21
00:00:52,288 --> 00:00:55,003
the value. So if you are storing a user

22
00:00:55,083 --> 00:00:57,957
object, in your variable, name the variable

23
00:00:58,134 --> 00:00:58,471
user.

24
00:00:59,114 --> 00:01:01,618
If you are storing an object which describes

25
00:01:01,618 --> 00:01:04,428
a database and offers database operations,

26
00:01:04,798 --> 00:01:07,190
you might wanna name the variable database.

27
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:11,172
If your value is a number or a string, so

28
00:01:11,172 --> 00:01:14,367
some text, then you also just wanna describe

29
00:01:14,367 --> 00:01:14,897
the value.

30
00:01:15,555 --> 00:01:18,205
If you have a variable that holds the name

31
00:01:18,205 --> 00:01:20,437
of a user, you might just wanna call it

32
00:01:20,629 --> 00:01:20,902
name.

33
00:01:21,512 --> 00:01:23,450
The same, of course, if it's a property in a

34
00:01:23,579 --> 00:01:24,428
user object.

35
00:01:25,277 --> 00:01:27,808
If you have a variable or property which

36
00:01:27,904 --> 00:01:30,291
holds an age, you might wanna call it age,

37
00:01:30,724 --> 00:01:31,493
just like that.

38
00:01:32,582 --> 00:01:34,857
Now for booleans, there is a special case.

39
00:01:35,674 --> 00:01:38,830
For booleans, your name should simply be a

40
00:01:38,958 --> 00:01:41,778
question that can be answered with true or

41
00:01:41,922 --> 00:01:42,307
false.

42
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:45,479
So you might name your variable something

43
00:01:45,543 --> 00:01:48,875
like isActive or loggedIn because that can

44
00:01:48,907 --> 00:01:51,919
be yes or no and therefore true or false.

45
00:01:52,352 --> 00:01:54,966
You are active or you are not. It's an

46
00:01:55,143 --> 00:01:58,189
either/or choice. So for Booleans, we want

47
00:01:58,189 --> 00:02:01,509
this is something naming or names like

48
00:02:01,637 --> 00:02:04,235
loggedIn, which also can be answered with

49
00:02:04,364 --> 00:02:05,117
yes or no.

50
00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:08,421
Now, you might wanna consider providing more

51
00:02:08,710 --> 00:02:10,361
details and more information

52
00:02:10,955 --> 00:02:13,440
where it makes sense, but you don't wanna

53
00:02:13,585 --> 00:02:15,413
introduce extra redundancy.

54
00:02:16,231 --> 00:02:18,973
For example, if your user in a certain

55
00:02:18,973 --> 00:02:21,250
branch of your code is always an

56
00:02:21,362 --> 00:02:23,767
authenticated user, you could consider

57
00:02:23,767 --> 00:02:26,620
naming that variable authenticatedUser to

58
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,880
make it really clear to everyone who reads

59
00:02:28,912 --> 00:02:31,524
that code that you're only acting on an

60
00:02:31,572 --> 00:02:32,774
authenticated user here.

61
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:35,082
If it's a customer, you could name it

62
00:02:35,082 --> 00:02:37,742
customer instead of user to have this more

63
00:02:37,742 --> 00:02:41,012
specific name. If it's an SQL database, you

64
00:02:41,012 --> 00:02:42,694
could use that as a name to make it really

65
00:02:42,694 --> 00:02:44,906
clear which kind of database you are working

66
00:02:44,938 --> 00:02:45,066
on.

67
00:02:46,204 --> 00:02:48,688
If you store just the first name of a user

68
00:02:48,704 --> 00:02:50,996
in a certain field, you might wanna name it

69
00:02:51,012 --> 00:02:53,609
firstName instead of just name to be more

70
00:02:53,609 --> 00:02:55,922
specific about the kind of name being stored

71
00:02:55,922 --> 00:02:56,243
in there.

72
00:02:56,870 --> 00:02:58,942
On the other hand, I would argue that age is

73
00:02:59,119 --> 00:03:01,512
already pretty specific, so you probably

74
00:03:01,544 --> 00:03:03,905
can't be more specific there. So it's not

75
00:03:03,969 --> 00:03:06,668
about making your names as long as possible,

76
00:03:06,925 --> 00:03:09,302
but just about making them as clear and as

77
00:03:09,559 --> 00:03:11,310
easy to understand as possible.

78
00:03:11,888 --> 00:03:14,088
And sometimes shorter is better, sometimes

79
00:03:14,217 --> 00:03:15,020
longer is better.

80
00:03:15,582 --> 00:03:17,735
Obviously, this is also something which will

81
00:03:17,767 --> 00:03:20,061
come with experience, but also with help of

82
00:03:20,093 --> 00:03:22,193
the demos and exercises you will see

83
00:03:22,257 --> 00:03:23,059
throughout this course.

84
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:26,633
Now for Booleans, it's the same. You can add

85
00:03:26,665 --> 00:03:29,326
more details if it makes sense. For example,

86
00:03:29,454 --> 00:03:32,387
if isActive refers to a user, you could name

87
00:03:32,435 --> 00:03:33,717
it isActiveUser.

88
00:03:34,262 --> 00:03:36,490
loggedIn on the other hand will typically

89
00:03:36,522 --> 00:03:39,135
always refer to a user. So for example,

90
00:03:39,247 --> 00:03:42,709
using userIsLoggedIn instead of just

91
00:03:42,773 --> 00:03:45,738
loggedIn is longer but not necessarily

92
00:03:45,818 --> 00:03:46,187
better.
